<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Metohija</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dukagjin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Metohija is the western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, a fertile basin drained by the White <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drin">Drin</a> that includes the towns of Peć, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjakova">Gjakova</a>. Its name comes from the Greek word for church estates, recalling the lands of medieval Serbian monasteries such as the Patriarchate of Peć. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> call the region Dukagjin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/metohija.jpg" alt="Metohija" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_geographical_regions_of_Kosovo.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
